#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048917 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050412 :topic/math-academy-id 3343 :topic/title "Vector Fields"}] :topic/id #uuid "cc0e7551-dafb-5e86-9c0e-20d3207eccdf" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171889 :question/id #uuid "f0111439-4fd8-5add-b95f-5e783178fd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-9440" :question/problem "Draw a diagram that represents the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n at the points $(-2,1), (-1,-2),$ and $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "To sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\,\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,1) & =-2(-2)(1)\\,i+(-2)^{2}\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-2) & =-2(-1)(-2)\\,i+(-1)^{2}\\,j=-4\\,i+j \\\\ F(1,-1) & =-2(1)(-1)\\,i+{1}^{2}\\,j=2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/cf/cf51cba8548d1f342a9dd5c06b533782feb955fca4b90adfac5b6988d7e2e470.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172084 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171890 :question/id #uuid "8f9615ef-b53e-51ee-9aa6-4df88afd6773" :question/math-academy-id "e-9441" :question/problem "![](images/29/2966d95bee0326ed5bf5a5fec9313d15bacd86334f9e9d144ff366bee63d9de3.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}$\n\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y) = -x \\, \\mathbf{i}$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y) = -x\\, \\mathbf{i}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171894 :question/id #uuid "b226c391-9538-59ff-85ff-dde4d73f209b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9473" :question/problem "![](images/de/de305db47bcfae58b75797e88ae4a7c7825174bd58d77991ba8dfb4c0b8a13cb.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf  F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3344 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049396 :topic/math-academy-id 3188 :topic/title "Phase Planes and Phase Portraits"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/math-academy-id 2131 :topic/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049617 :topic/math-academy-id 3717 :topic/title "Stream Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885 :topic/math-academy-id 3692 :topic/title "Gradient Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/math-academy-id 2132 :topic/title "The Curl of a Vector Field"}] :topic/title "Visualizing Vector Fields"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050173 :topic/math-academy-id 1166 :topic/title "Three-Dimensional Vectors in Component Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050938 :topic/math-academy-id 243 :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Angle and Magnitude"}] :topic/id #uuid "cee13705-0b60-5ea0-b11e-46fd35bede4e" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059730 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059543 :question/id #uuid "1ab8245a-39fb-5633-b5ff-865ab17dbfb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4323" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 0,-0.4,11 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,5,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2+(-0.4)\\cdot 5+11\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-2+22 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using the General Formula"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059731 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059544 :question/id #uuid "dfc10727-c0d7-5b83-a4f6-afe3eb4831cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4324" :question/problem "Let $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$. Given that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, find the value of $t$." :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot t \\\\  & =-8-10+t \\\\  & =t-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given Two Perpendicular Vectors"}] :topic/math-academy-id 177 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048233 :topic/math-academy-id 1738 :topic/title "Differentiation Rules for Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297 :topic/math-academy-id 147 :topic/title "Multiplying Square Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377 :topic/math-academy-id 1792 :topic/title "Tangent Vectors and Tangent Lines to Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049291 :topic/math-academy-id 1741 :topic/title "Limits of Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436 :topic/math-academy-id 1805 :topic/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049663 :topic/math-academy-id 1744 :topic/title "Integration Rules for Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814 :topic/math-academy-id 1849 :topic/title "Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853 :topic/math-academy-id 2108 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions Over Parametric Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050336 :topic/math-academy-id 1278 :topic/title "The Angle Between Two Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825 :topic/math-academy-id 1195 :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector"}] :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Components"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673 :topic/math-academy-id 66 :topic/title "Solving Two-Step Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/math-academy-id 861 :topic/title "Introduction to Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049026 :topic/math-academy-id 352 :topic/title "Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050208 :topic/math-academy-id 362 :topic/title "The Power Rule for Exponents With Algebraic Expressions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017 :topic/math-academy-id 353 :topic/title "Factoring Polynomials Using GCFs"}] :topic/id #uuid "d58eda6d-18a9-51fc-a2ae-3720429fd25b" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059557 :question/id #uuid "2b6e8845-a53d-510c-b44f-1b00271ee507" :question/math-academy-id "e-4434" :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A),$ if $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix With Integer Entries"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059558 :question/id #uuid "b066b73a-c777-550c-9c6e-af8093bf7fab" :question/math-academy-id "e-4435" :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A),$ if $[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}-(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix With Non-Integer Entries"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059559 :question/id #uuid "043a5e58-e1a0-50a5-b1be-d404e6262991" :question/math-academy-id "e-4436" :question/problem "If\n${A} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -1 \\\n        -3 & k\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack$\nand\n$\\det({A}) = 5$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =5 \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in an Expression Involving the Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 152 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048857 :topic/math-academy-id 1169 :topic/title "The Geometric Interpretation of the 2x2 Determinant"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048965 :topic/math-academy-id 233 :topic/title "The Minors of a 3x3 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548 :topic/math-academy-id 864 :topic/title "Inverses of 2x2 Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050129 :topic/math-academy-id 1775 :topic/title "Cramer's Rule for 2x2 Systems of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150 :topic/math-academy-id 2547 :topic/title "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs"}] :topic/title "The Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303 :topic/math-academy-id 1038 :topic/title "Integration Using the Double-Angle Formulas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049321 :topic/math-academy-id 4135 :topic/title "Polar Coordinate Transformations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/math-academy-id 2151 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050546 :topic/math-academy-id 2000 :topic/title "Properties of Double Integrals"}] :topic/id #uuid "d952f888-5e27-5f27-a3ff-b83b56acd32a" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129016 :question/id #uuid "d3ae6c85-14bd-57ad-b8cc-1cebb53683b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7346" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{[{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{2}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(\\pi -2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129017 :question/id #uuid "059deff9-dfa3-5c3e-88b1-194af41d3ed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7347" :question/problem "![](images/17/1782385c55493892be0308ede1e4fbe5308ea9440e21b70cc2da7d7f01fb8dcd.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129018 :question/id #uuid "f31dd085-70fa-5eed-adf9-89fb0e6654e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7348" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/05/051cfbbf3fd37f01ff7e4442fccdc5ec4ce506e3c452b68bfa2ed5ae6e5cf6b9.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({2}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32({sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{3}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128853 :question/id #uuid "e46cfe46-965d-5c28-90ed-060ec39751a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12376" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/14/14df7999d11afbe9002e05521d71f5008f2b49250238a714fada7dcc4aeb86d2.svg) express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](images/00/009865419103717553af1a73b3de0ac958a76eb2fdc9c95467236581d3ad0e11.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n![](images/82/829d32a5563a183ecc1f646b20fba584838dffb9ce67dcc0e014d2a90bcb7add.svg)\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region ![](images/f7/f7c8d853846850d1bf4afd67f7544f3f3c3756eb0de33ee5c27985e83449997d.svg)\n\n![](images/e4/e447e688e0f3e2c0411129e8d1baf4a900008173443f5392d98af52a27eb285b.png)\n\nNotice that the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) represents the disk of radius ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) centered at ![](images/37/37cb790b6a640626cb093bf7ee969ddc9aafd1eb91c86d96a5339e6df2555fca.svg) \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n![](images/df/dfe1b368d21a4745d71b5fe084246ce0a16cb6a85ad983598442d0dbde0ed591.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ![](images/ae/ae3d9424cc536ab651dc8732546bd8bfea96901ce744793ec8797c02fc446ba4.svg) in polar coordinates:\n![](images/11/11d7065eda072cefb2c9ab63e679d3a0e7103a146a37b21440f0ff2e4f393369.svg) \n\nSince ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have ![](images/3f/3f7ce8399dec23cec965849d4623642dcaf947ca8500a837ce8ef771a42b7ee3.svg)\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) and ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) First of all, ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) can vary from ![](images/63/632079982c24b29d4de5e322230ed61f106b1715de40fdb8b6879e982a9f3955.svg) (at the origin) to ![](images/4b/4b56a88cc6e7ba7c13d83cf18ea0f759a70af4d07186f2be1c558e25589e1c7c.svg) (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/52/522df0e95e36cd67f34ffcc0ec4757c35749eab5d5137215c456c8b7e90994ae.png)\n\nSecondly, ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) can vary between ![](images/64/64fd2741e70e8f1d2a64c58d05dc7124c6e6e94a5d7dea30124e4200e8c61e75.svg) (the negative ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis) and ![](images/b7/b76ea8adee01315895dd8c61949a5c8f28235929b53bbff59c58856e04ebd63f.svg) (the positive ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis):\n\n![](images/6a/6ad24b638877941296df25869f8b0ee6f9dc1e73a779a8a0da01de35d9f74f42.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates ![](images/49/4974a18e033f45691b0d3cab8bd7a16744004e4303f44184bb7045a7c64e6f8c.svg) the region can be expressed as \n![](images/9c/9cab9c4a9d446ee9fd9794a9455d46b63df1b53ce505236d7b91816cc6496890.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n![](images/5d/5d809200294cb2ddc88d35101eb6fc449902fce4ebb0b0fd9c883a32ee5c3b0c.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Not Centered at the Origin"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2030 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049267 :topic/math-academy-id 2025 :topic/title "Density, Mass, and Charge of Plane Laminas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049883 :topic/math-academy-id 1264 :topic/title "Surface Integrals Over Cartesian Surfaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957 :topic/math-academy-id 3147 :topic/title "The Average Value of a Multivariable Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332 :topic/math-academy-id 3627 :topic/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050343 :topic/math-academy-id 2059 :topic/title "Triple Integrals in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050816 :topic/math-academy-id 3602 :topic/title "Areas of Surfaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016 :topic/math-academy-id 2835 :topic/title "Double Integrals Between Polar Curves"}] :topic/title "Double Integrals in Plane Polar Coordinates"}]}
